description:
User idea on 2026-10-09: "In a chat, I want to be able to add multiple selected papers. Then this chat will appear in all papers it includes." Today a chat belongs to exactly one paper and one PDF version (chat_sessions.paper_id/document_id), retrieval runs over one document, and citation validation requires every citation to belong to that document. Lead assessment: doable but cross-cutting.
- Schema: a chat-paper link table with a pinned document per paper, an Alembic migration and a backfill; the first schema change since 0007.
- Listing: the chat appears in every included paper's list (easy once the link exists).
- Composer: the existing @ PaperPicker gains multi-select and adding papers mid-chat (easy).
- Retrieval: search runs across several documents with per-paper evidence quotas, so one long paper cannot crowd out others, and per-paper embedding profiles. This is the hard part.
- Citations: validation accepts any document in the chat, and chips name their paper.
- Reader: a citation to another paper switches the reader to that PDF.
- Local models: the ~3k-token local input budget splits across papers, so the 7B answers degrade.
Proposed defaults:
- add papers via @ at the start or mid-chat;
- removal is allowed and history keeps its citation snapshots;
- a fair per-paper evidence share;
- chips like 'Smith 2023 · p. 4';
- at most 5 papers, driven by the local budget;
- a paper-chip header with per-paper summaries on demand instead of stacked summary cards.
Throughput-based estimate: about 1.5-2.5 h of one Opus agent (ARA-084 took ~65 min, ARA-086 ~90 min), plus browser verification. Status: not started; the user is deciding whether to add it.
Update on 2026-10-09: the user moved open models behind an experimental flag (ARA-090), so the local-budget constraint is out of scope, and asked that cross-paper search follow industry-standard multi-document RAG. The standard pattern, as in LlamaIndex's multi-document guides, has several parts:
- one shared index built with a single embedding model, filtered by metadata to the chat's documents;
- hybrid lexical and dense retrieval with reciprocal rank fusion, then reranking over the union;
- per-document coverage, a quota or MMR, so one paper cannot crowd out the others;
- query decomposition for comparative questions: the planner emits per-paper sub-questions, each retrieved within its paper, then one synthesis step, in the Sub-Question Query Engine pattern;
- answers cite each paper explicitly.
Embedding vectors from different models (for example text-embedding-3-small and -large) live in different spaces and must not be mixed in one search. Multi-paper chats therefore need every included paper indexed with the same model, which favours one library-wide embedding model over the per-paper override.
User decisions on 2026-10-09:
- start after ARA-090 lands (and ARA-091, which provides the library-wide embedding model);
- reader tabs: clicking a citation from another paper opens that PDF in a new tab in the reader, and the user can close tabs;
- routing: a multi-paper chat lives at /papers/{first paper in the chat's list}/chats/{chatId}.
User request on 2026-10-10: at any time in a chat, add or remove papers with the same @ picker as the home page.
Design on 2026-10-10: follow docs/plans/retrieval-orchestration.md, steps 1–8 for several papers. Retrieval always; budgets come from the chat model's catalog window (ARA-093, built in parallel). Per-paper floor of about 2 passages, then relevance; routing to the most relevant papers when they exceed what fits; sub-questions for comparisons. One implementation, no flags. ARA-094 later adds pruning, reordering, the wider retry and section summaries.
Implemented on 2026-10-10: chat_papers link table (migration 0010) with a last-paper trigger; POST /chats, POST/DELETE /chats/{id}/papers and GET /chats/{id}; scoped hybrid retrieval with per-document top k, planner sub-questions for comparisons, a two-passage floor per paper with relevance routing and grouped evidence headers; paper-named citation chips, the @ picker in every composer, paper chips with summaries on demand and reader tabs. Map-reduce for small windows was not built because local models are refused in multi-paper chats while frozen.
Scope correction on 2026-10-10: the user removed the paper cap (the 5 came from the lead's local-model reasoning). The implementation was reworked for any number of papers: bounded lists, batched queries, named papers kept, at most five sub-questions, a bounded planner list, and chips and tabs that overflow.
